.Sh DESCRIPTION
This is the C interface to the engine: in-process models written as
pure C step functions.
It is the engine's calibration path, and the way to embed the checker
in a program of your own.
To check a program as it ships, write a model file and run
.Nm cmc Fl -model-file
instead; it needs nothing from this interface
.Po
.Xr cmc 1 ,
.Xr cmc 7
.Pc .
.Fn cmc_main
accepts
.Fl -model-file
too, so a program that calls it is a full
.Xr cmc 1 .
.Pp
An in-process model is protocol logic written as pure C step functions.
The product calls those functions to run the protocol; libcmc links the
same object files and explores every order in which messages, timers,
disk writes, client requests and crashes can reach them.
.Xr cmc 7
describes the model, and
.Xr cmc 1
the command line.
.Pp
A program supplies three things:
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
A
.Vt CmcCore :
the step code and the size of a node's state.
.It
A
.Vt CmcModel :
a core plus the properties to check against it.
.It
A
.Fn main
that calls
.Fn cmc_main ,
or that drives
.Fn cmc_run_new
and
.Fn cmc_check
itself.
.El
.Ss The core
.Bd -literal
typedef struct CmcCore {
	const char *name;
	size_t	state_size;	/* bytes per node */
	size_t	durable_size;	/* leading bytes that survive a crash */
	size_t	cfg_size;	/* at most 256 */
	void	(*cfg_default)(void *cfg);
	int	(*cfg_set)(void *cfg, const char *key, const char *val);
	int	(*nnodes)(const void *cfg);
	void	(*init)(const void *cfg, int node, void *state);
	void	(*step)(const void *cfg, int node, void *state,
		    const CmcEvent *ev, CmcOut *out);
	void	(*apply_persist)(const void *cfg, int node, void *durable,
		    const uint8_t *body, uint16_t len);
	void	(*describe)(const void *cfg, int node, const void *state,
		    FILE *f);			/* optional */
	void	(*describe_msg)(const void *cfg, const uint8_t *body,
		    FILE *f);			/* optional */
	int	(*direct_actions)(const void *cfg, const void *state,
		    CmcAction *acts, int max);	/* calibration only */
	void	(*describe_action)(const void *cfg, CmcAction a,
		    FILE *f);			/* calibration only */
	int	(*clients)(const void *cfg, int node);	/* optional */
	int	(*crashable)(const void *cfg, int node);	/* optional */
	int	(*time_fields)(const void *cfg, int node, size_t *off,
		    int max);			/* optional */
} CmcCore;
.Ed
.Pp
A node's state is
.Fa state_size
bytes, fixed, with no pointers.
The engine copies, compares and hashes it as bytes, so the core keeps
it canonical: padding and unused slots zeroed, sets sorted.
.Pp
.Fn cfg_default
fills the core's settings;
.Fn cfg_set
applies one
.Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value
and returns 0, or \-1 to reject it.
.Fn nnodes
returns the number of nodes, 1 to
.Dv CMC_MAX_NODES
(8).
.Fn init
sets a node's initial state.
.Pp
.Fn step
handles one event at one node.
It must be total and deterministic, and may read only its arguments: no
clock, randomness, globals, I/O or allocation.
It changes the state in place and emits effects with
.Fn cmc_out_push .
It must not change the first
.Fa durable_size
bytes; only
.Fn apply_persist ,
which the engine calls when a persist reaches disk, may change them.
A core with a non-zero
.Fa durable_size
must supply
.Fn apply_persist .
.Pp
.Fn clients ,
if set, says which nodes take client requests; it reads the settings,
never the state.
.Fn crashable ,
if set, says which nodes may crash, also from the settings alone:
.Dv CMC_A_CRASH
is never offered for a node it excludes.
.Dv NULL
means every node may crash.
.Pp
.Fn time_fields ,
if set, stores in
.Fa off
the offsets of the u64 times a node keeps in its state (readings of its
own clock, such as a lease's end), at most
.Fa max
of them
.Pq Dv CMC_MAX_TIME_FIELDS , 16 ,
inside the volatile part, and returns how many; it reads the settings,
never the state.
A core that sets it must run with
.Fa time_bound :
the engine then stores each node's times relative to its clock and
saturates past times older than the bound, so worlds that differ only
in when things happened merge.
The core's obligation is that
.Fn step
depends on time only through
.Fa ev->now
and its declared fields, would behave the same with all of them moved
by one constant, compares a past time only with constants up to the
bound, and sends no time in a message or a persist record.
.Fa time_bound
is refused for a model with a history variable, whose bytes the engine
could not move with the clocks.
.Fa time_check
tests the obligation on every step the search takes; see
.Sx Time fields
in
.Xr cmc 7 .
.Fn describe
and
.Fn describe_msg
print states and message bodies in traces.
.Fn direct_actions
and
.Fn describe_action
exist for calibration cores that port a shared-memory TLA+ specification
.Pq Xr cmc 7 ;
a message-passing core leaves them
.Dv NULL .
.Ss Events and effects
.Fn step
receives a
.Vt CmcEvent :
.Bd -literal
typedef struct CmcEvent {
	uint8_t	kind;		/* CmcEventKind */
	uint8_t	src;		/* RECV, CONN_RESET: the peer */
	uint16_t len;		/* RECV, FLUSHED: bytes in body */
	uint32_t arg;		/* TIMER, WAKE: the id; CLIENT: request number */
	CmcAction action;	/* ACTION (calibration only) */
	const uint8_t *body;	/* RECV: the message; FLUSHED: the record */
	uint32_t arg2;		/* TIMER, WAKE: the argument */
	uint64_t now;		/* this node's clock */
} CmcEvent;
.Ed
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width CMC_EV_CONN_RESET -compact
.It Dv CMC_EV_RECV
A message from
.Fa src .
.It Dv CMC_EV_TIMER
Timer
.Fa arg
fired;
.Fa arg2
is the argument it was armed with.
.It Dv CMC_EV_FLUSHED
The persist record in
.Fa body
is now durable.
.It Dv CMC_EV_WAKE
Waiter
.Fa arg
was woken;
.Fa arg2
is the argument of the wake.
.It Dv CMC_EV_CLIENT
A client request,
.Fa arg
counting from 0 at this node.
.It Dv CMC_EV_RESTART
The node crashed: its volatile bytes are zeroed and it is starting
again.
.It Dv CMC_EV_CONN_RESET
The connection to
.Fa src
was reset, and the messages on it are gone.
.It Dv CMC_EV_ACTION
Calibration cores only.
.El
.Pp
.Fn cmc_out_push
appends an effect of the given kind to
.Fa out ,
zeroes it and returns it for the core to fill in.
.Bl -tag -width CMC_OUT_CANCEL_TIMER -compact
.It Dv CMC_OUT_SEND
Send
.Fa body
.Pq Fa len No bytes, at most Dv CMC_MSG_MAX , No 32
to
.Fa dst .
.It Dv CMC_OUT_SET_TIMER
Arm timer
.Fa arg ,
0 to 255, with argument
.Fa arg2 ,
and duration
.Fa dur .
The pair names the timer: arming it again while armed restarts it, and
the same id with another argument is another timer.
A timer with
.Fa dur
0 is untimed and may fire at any point.
With
.Fa dur
> 0 it fires when the node's clock reaches
.Fa now
+
.Fa dur ,
and only when that is the node's earliest deadline; firing moves the
clocks
.Pq Xr cmc 7 .
Only
.Dv CMC_OUT_SET_TIMER
may carry a duration.
At most
.Fa timer_cap
timers are armed on a node at once.
.It Dv CMC_OUT_CANCEL_TIMER
Disarm timer
.Fa arg
with argument
.Fa arg2 .
.It Dv CMC_OUT_PERSIST
Queue
.Fa body
as a disk write.
.It Dv CMC_OUT_WAKE
Queue a wake of waiter
.Fa arg ,
0 to 255, with argument
.Fa arg2 .
As for timers, the pair names the wake, and at most
.Fa wake_cap
are pending on a node at once.
.It Dv CMC_OUT_REPLY
Answer a client with
.Fa arg .
The engine appends the reply to the ghost log that properties read.
With the request log on
.Pq Fa requests No in the model ,
.Fa req
names the request the reply answers: 1 plus the request's number at
this node, the
.Fa arg
of its
.Dv CMC_EV_CLIENT .
0 names none, and the reply goes to the ghost log only.
A reply that names a request never invoked, answers one twice, or
names one when the model keeps no log is an engine violation.
.El
.Pp
A core never writes
.Fa out->o
directly; the purity gate rejects source that does.
Past
.Dv CMC_OUT_MAX
(16) effects in one step,
.Fn cmc_out_push
returns a scratch entry and the engine reports the overflow as a
violation.
.Ss Properties
A property is a
.Vt CmcProp ,
made with one of the macros below from a name and a function.
State properties have the type
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bool fn(const CmcWorld *w, CmcWhy *why);
.Ed
.Pp
and action properties
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bool fn(const CmcWorld *pre, CmcAction a, const CmcWorld *post,
    CmcWhy *why);
.Ed
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width CMC_ACTION_PROPERTY -compact
.It Fn CMC_INVARIANT
Holds in every reachable state.
.It Fn CMC_ACTION_PROPERTY
Holds on every transition, including those to states already seen.
.It Fn CMC_WITNESS_PROP
Holds in some reachable state.
A witness never reached fails the run: it means the other properties
may hold only because the interesting states were never reached.
.It Fn CMC_QUIESCENT_PROP
Holds in every state where nothing can happen, or where the only
possible actions are timers that fire and change nothing.
.It Fn CMC_EVENTUALLY_PROP
Bounded liveness: from every reachable state, the fair schedule (no
faults, no new clients) reaches a state where it holds within
.Va live_steps
steps.
Each round drains the pending messages before it fires the armed
timers, but cores that keep messaging each other in a cycle cannot
postpone a timer.
The verdict is about this one schedule, not every fair one.
.El
.Pp
Each macro fills in a
.Vt CmcProp
whose kind is
.Dv CMC_INV ,
.Dv CMC_ACTION_PROP ,
.Dv CMC_WITNESS ,
.Dv CMC_QUIESCENT
or
.Dv CMC_EVENTUALLY .
.Pp
A property returns
.Dv false
to report a violation, and may explain it with
.Fn cmc_why ,
which formats like
.Xr printf 3 .
.Pp
Properties read the world through accessors.
.Fn cmc_node
returns node
.Fa node Ns 's
state and
.Fn cmc_cfg
the core's settings.
.Fn cmc_nreplies
and
.Fn cmc_reply
read the ghost log of client replies, in the order they were emitted.
.Fn cmc_requests
returns the number of client requests delivered to a node, counted by
the environment rather than by the core.
.Fn cmc_clock
returns a node's clock and
.Fn cmc_now
the reference time, in the core's time unit; both are 0 for a
calibration core and until a timed timer fires.
.Pp
With
.Fa requests
set in the model, the world also holds the request log.
.Fn cmc_nrequests
returns the number of requests invoked so far; request
.Fa i
runs from 0, ordered by node and then by request number.
.Fn cmc_request
returns its node, its number at that node, whether a reply has named it,
and that reply's
.Fa arg .
.Fn cmc_precedes
is true when request
.Fa i
was answered before request
.Fa j
was invoked: the real-time order a linearizability check needs.
A reply in the step that invokes a request does not precede it.
The log records this order and no step numbers, so two paths to the
same log still merge.
Calling these on a model without the log, or with
.Fa i
out of range, prints the reason and exits with status 3: a property
that read an empty log would pass for the wrong reason.
.Pp
.Fn cmc_history
returns the model's history bytes
.Pq Fa history_size No of them , No or NULL if it has none .
They start at zero, and after every step the engine calls the model's
.Fn history
with the worlds before and after it and the bytes to update, which
start as the bytes before the step.
They are part of the world: the fingerprint covers them, so two paths
that reach the same core states with different histories are two
states.
Cores never see them, so a history variable cannot change behaviour.
.Fn history
must be deterministic; under the determinism gate it runs twice.
Properties may read core state; the engine never calls them to decide
which actions are possible.
.Ss Version
.Dv CMC_VERSION
is the release as a string,
.Ql MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH ,
following semantic versioning.
A change to
.In cmc.h
that breaks a core, a model or a caller written against an earlier
release of the same major version is not made outside a major release;
before 1.0.0, a minor release may make one, and
.Pa CHANGELOG.md
says so.
.Ss The model
.Bd -literal
typedef struct CmcModel {
	const char *name;
	const CmcCore *core;
	const CmcProp *props;
	int	nprops;
	int	nalways;	/* the first nalways props are always checked */
	bool	requests;	/* keep the request log */
	size_t	history_size;	/* bytes of history variables */
	void	(*history)(const CmcWorld *pre, CmcAction a,
			   const CmcWorld *post, uint8_t *ghost);
} CmcModel;

typedef struct CmcModelEntry {
	const char *name;	/* the --model name */
	const CmcModel *m;
} CmcModelEntry;
.Ed
.Ss Actions
A
.Vt CmcAction
is a 64-bit word.
.Fn CMC_ACT kind a b c d
packs one;
.Fn CMC_ACT_KIND ,
.Fn CMC_ACT_A ,
.Fn CMC_ACT_B ,
.Fn CMC_ACT_C
and
.Fn CMC_ACT_D
unpack its kind (the top byte), three byte fields and a 32-bit field.
An action property receives the action between its two worlds.
In a message-passing core the kind is one of
.Bl -tag -width CMC_A_DELIVER -compact
.It Dv CMC_A_DELIVER
a = destination, b = source, d = index of the message in the network.
.It Dv CMC_A_DROP
The same fields; the message is lost.
.It Dv CMC_A_DUP
The same fields; the message is duplicated.
.It Dv CMC_A_FIRE
a = node, b = timer.
.It Dv CMC_A_FLUSH
a = node: its oldest pending persist completes.
.It Dv CMC_A_WAKE
a = node, b = waiter.
.It Dv CMC_A_CLIENT
a = node.
.It Dv CMC_A_CRASH
a = node, b = how many pending persists reached disk.
.It Dv CMC_A_RESET
a = node, b = the peer whose connection was reset.
.El
.Pp
A calibration core defines its own kinds.
.Ss Options
.Bd -literal
typedef struct CmcOptions {
	CmcSearch search;	/* CMC_BFS, CMC_DFS, CMC_SIMULATE, CMC_REPLAY */
	int	max_depth;	/* DFS bound; simulation walk length */
	uint64_t seed;		/* simulation: first seed */
	uint64_t walks;		/* simulation: number of walks */
	uint64_t max_states;	/* 0: none; reaching it is incomplete */
	double	time_limit;	/* seconds; 0: none */
	bool	det;		/* determinism gate */
	bool	stop_on_witness; /* BFS: stop once every witness is
				    reached; the result is incomplete */
	bool	quiet;		/* print no trace or progress */
	bool	coverage;	/* count states per action kind */
	int	progress_s;	/* progress line period; 0: off */
	const char *trace_out;	/* write a replay file on a violation */
	const char *replay_in;	/* CMC_REPLAY: the file */
	int	client_budget, drop_budget, dup_budget, crash_budget;
	int	net_cap, persist_cap, reply_cap;
	int	timer_cap, wake_cap;	/* per node */
	int	drift;		/* clock drift, per thousand, 0 to 500 */
	uint64_t max_time;	/* 0: none; past it is incomplete */
	uint64_t time_bound;	/* 0: off; the largest time difference
				    the core compares against */
	bool	time_check;	/* step again with times shifted */
	int	req_cap;	/* request log slots, at most 64 */
	CmcNet	net;		/* CMC_NET_FIFO or CMC_NET_BAG */
	int	live_steps;	/* bound for eventually-properties */
	FILE	*out;		/* progress output; NULL: stdout */
} CmcOptions;
.Ed
.Pp
The fields are the options of
.Xr cmc 1 ,
which gives their defaults.
.Ss Running a check
.Fn cmc_main
parses the command line described in
.Xr cmc 1 ,
runs the check, prints TLC-style output, and returns the verdict, which
the caller returns from
.Fn main .
.Pp
To drive the engine directly, fill a
.Vt CmcOptions
with
.Fn cmc_options_default ,
change what is needed, and call
.Fn cmc_run_new
with the model, a settings string
.Ql key=value,...
and a
.Dv NULL Ns -terminated
list of property names, or
.Dv NULL
for all of them.
On failure it returns
.Dv NULL
and writes the reason to
.Fa err .
.Pp
.Fn cmc_check
runs the search and fills
.Fa res .
On a violation,
.Fa res->prop
names the property
.Po
.Ql cmc:engine
for a broken engine rule
.Pc ,
.Fa res->why
explains it, and
.Fa res->trace
holds the
.Fa res->trace_len No \- 1
actions from the initial state.
.Fn cmc_print_trace
prints them state by state, and
.Fn cmc_write_replay
saves them in the format
.Fl -replay
reads.
.Fn cmc_result_free
frees the trace;
.Fn cmc_run_free
frees the run.
.Pp
.Fn cmc_world_size
returns the bytes in one world: every node's state plus the environment.
.Fn cmc_describe_action
prints one action.
.Ss Lower level
.Fn cmc_world_init
writes the initial world into
.Fa w ,
which holds
.Fn cmc_world_size
bytes.
.Fn cmc_enabled
lists the actions possible in
.Fa w
and returns their number, or \-1 if there are more than
.Fa max .
.Fn cmc_apply
applies one action in place.
.Fn cmc_fingerprint
is the 128-bit hash the visited-state table uses.
These exist for the engine's own tests.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn cmc_main
returns a
.Vt CmcVerdict :
.Dv CMC_PASS
(0),
.Dv CMC_VIOLATION
(1),
.Dv CMC_INCOMPLETE
(2) or
.Dv CMC_ERROR
(3), the exit statuses listed in
.Xr cmc 1 .
.Pp
.Fn cmc_run_new
returns
.Dv NULL
on a bad setting, an unknown property, a bound out of range, a
durable part without
.Fn apply_persist ,
a duplicating FIFO network, a
.Fa search
that is none of the four, a
.Dv CMC_REPLAY
search with no
.Fa replay_in ,
or a
.Dv CMC_SIMULATE
search with
.Fa walks
of 0.
.Fn cmc_apply
returns 0, or \-1 if the action is not possible or a core broke an
engine rule.
.Fn cmc_write_replay
returns 0, or \-1 if the file cannot be written.
.Sh EXAMPLES
A complete model, with the core, two properties and
.Fn main ,
is in
.Pa docs/_includes/snippets/quickstart.c
in the source tree.
The skeleton:
.Bd -literal
#include "cmc.h"

static const CmcCore my_core = {
	.name = "mine",
	.state_size = sizeof(MyState),
	.durable_size = offsetof(MyState, first_volatile),
	.cfg_default = my_cfg_default,
	.cfg_set = my_cfg_set,
	.nnodes = my_nnodes,
	.init = my_init,
	.step = my_step,
	.apply_persist = my_apply_persist,
};

static const CmcProp my_props[] = {
	CMC_INVARIANT(NoLostAck, no_lost_ack),
	CMC_WITNESS_PROP(Acked, acked),
};

static const CmcModel my_model = {
	.name = "mine", .core = &my_core,
	.props = my_props, .nprops = 2,
};

int
main(int argc, char **argv)
{
	static const CmcModelEntry models[] = {
		{"mine", &my_model},
	};

	return cmc_main(argc, argv, models, 1);
}
.Ed
.Pp
Build it against the library:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
cc -std=c11 -Ilibcmc/include mine.c mine_core.o \e
    libcmc/build/libcmc.a -o mycmc
.Ed
.Sh LIMITS
.Bl -tag -width CMC_MAX_ACTIONS -compact
.It Dv CMC_MAX_NODES
8 nodes.
.It Dv CMC_MSG_MAX
32 bytes per message or persist record.
.It Dv CMC_OUT_MAX
16 effects per step.
.It Dv CMC_MAX_ACTIONS
2048 possible actions per state.
.It Dv CMC_MAX_TIMERS
16, the default
.Fa timer_cap :
armed timers per node.
.It Dv CMC_MAX_WAKES
8, the default
.Fa wake_cap :
pending wakes per node.
.It Properties
64 of each kind in one run;
.Fn cmc_run_new
refuses more.
.El
.Pp
Going past a limit is reported as a violation, never truncated.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cmc 1 ,
